    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Equivalent fractions",
     "md": "Fractions that show the **same amount** are **equivalent**: $\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{4}{8}$. To make an equivalent fraction, **multiply** (or divide) the numerator and the denominator by the **same number**. With the same denominator, the bigger numerator is the bigger fraction: $\\frac{3}{8} > \\frac{2}{8}$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- The parts must be **equal**: a rectangle cut in 3 unequal parts does not show thirds.\n- Thinking that a bigger denominator means a bigger fraction: $\\frac{1}{8}$ is smaller than $\\frac{1}{4}$.\n- Changing only the top or only the bottom number when finding an equivalent fraction."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Write the fraction of a cake that is eaten when 3 of 8 equal slices are eaten.",
     "steps": [
      "The cake has 8 equal parts: the denominator is 8.",
      "3 parts are eaten: the numerator is 3."
     ],
     "answer": "3/8 of the cake.",
